61fb72cc-01d2-41b2-bb88-34977265c621	Growth Standup	2026-02-05	61	{"Harsh Sharma","Rohan Kumar","Seema Samanta"}	/root/transcriptor/meetingassistant/transcripts/merged/2026-02-05_20-00_growth-standup__meet-recording-446f8965-d40f-4a63-af40-0167b5c5587b.json	\N	2026-02-05 16:03:41.59	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"analysis": {"topics": ["Luxury beauty market size and growth in India (estimated at 0.8 billion)", "CAGR predictions for the luxury beauty market", "<unused5784> trends in luxury beauty purchases (mini/travel size products)", "Regional variations in luxury beauty demand (Tier 1, 2, and 3 cities)", "Specific regional preferences (cleansers/toners in NCR, sunscreens in Southern India)", "Product classification in the luxury beauty market (fragrances, perfumes)", "Male vs. female spending split in the luxury fragrance market (80/20)", "The impact of male income on fragrance market share", "Emerging brands attracting female customers in the fragrance market", "The role of gifting in luxury beauty purchases (men buying for women)", "Exploring new product categories to cater to male customers", "Potential male-specific product categories (solid perfume, direct perfumes, aftershave)", "The team's current product categories (four existing categories)", "Market research and data analysis for informed decision-making", "Course correction of product strategy based on new findings", "Review of a pitch deck and associated documentation.", "Discussion of the Indian market and positioning strategy.", "Clarification of the 'dashboard thing' for Harsh.", "Importance of understanding the positioning of the product/service.", "Seema offering support and clarification to Harsh.", "Reference to a document with answers to questions.", "Review of the 'sources of gtm tab' and a specific link (luxasia.com).", "The process of investigation and learning from successes and failures.", "The importance of documentation and knowledge sharing.", "Checking in on Harsh's understanding.", "The concept of a 'dashboard' (details unclear)."], "summary": [{"topic": "Market Segmentation and Cohort Definition", "points": ["Discussion centers on defining customer segments for targeting, specifically focusing on income levels and purchasing power.", "Initial discussion on the percentage of women in billionaire/millionaire categories (12-18%, aiming for 20-25% for realistic representation).", "Debate on whether to increase the 8% representation of aspirational premium buyers due to new data.", "Clarification that the 8% previously represented income above 10 lakhs, while the 3% represents above 20 lakhs.", "Seema suggests needing to define the percentage of people with income between 5-15 lakhs to accurately represent the aspirational premium segment.", "Reference to a survey indicating 80% of the population has a PCI (per capita income) around 1.2-1.3 lakhs.", "Discussion of the validity of the 80% PCI figure, acknowledging the vast income disparity in India.", "Emphasis on the importance of disposable income as a better indicator of purchasing power.", "Debate on whether to adjust the percentages based on new data and ensure mutually exclusive cohorts.", "Seema wants to define the percentage of people earning between 5-15 lakhs.", "Reference to a survey showing 7-8% of the population has income above 10 lakhs and 70-72% below 5 lakhs.", " regering to the article stating that only the top 10% of the population falls in the income bracket wherein their net worth is more than 25 lakh rupees.", "Discussion about the difference between net worth and disposable income.", "Harsh offered to share census data on income.", "Rohan emphasizes the importance of defining a smaller, well-defined cohort for targeted marketing.", "Seema wants to determine the percentage of people with income above 15 lakhs.", "Discussion about the importance of defining mutually exclusive cohorts for accurate targeting."], "end_time": "22:26", "start_time": "11:54", "time_range": "11:54 - 22:26", "participants": []}, {"topic": "Initial Cohort Guesstimation & Income Disparity", "points": ["Discussion began with questioning the accuracy of income percentages, specifically the claim that 80% of the population earns less than 8,000 rupees, deeming it potentially too high.", "Seema presented initial data breakdown: 80-85% earning under 5 lakhs, 7-8% between 5-10 lakhs, and 8-10% earning over 10 lakhs.", "Concern raised about the reliability of the data, with a need to verify the numbers against current statistics.", "Discussion of adjusting income brackets, specifically changing the 15% earning over 10 lakhs to 20% and the 60% bracket to 25儚 to better represent female representation in the cohorts.", "Reference to articles stating 80% of Indians earn less than 15,000 per month, emphasizing the need for source verification and understanding the calculation methods.", "Acknowledgement that data is potentially 5 years old, necessitating updated information.", "Harsh asked for clarification on the data's meaning and understanding of the income brackets.", "Discussion of the median income in India being around 18,000.", "Concerns about picking numbers arbitrarily and the need for supporting data.", "Seema clarified that the goal is to refine the income distribution to better reflect the target audience."], "end_time": "26:38", "start_time": "22:28", "time_range": "22:28 - 26:38", "participants": []}, {"topic": "Defining Cohort Characteristics & Overlap", "points": ["Discussion of the characteristics defining each cohort, focusing on affluent, sustainability-driven, and impact-oriented consumers.", "Seema clarified that the 'Conscious Lifestyle Adopter' (Cohort 3) is potentially a subset of the affluent (Cohort 1) and regular customers (Cohort 2).", "Rohan suggested that Cohort 3 might be becoming redundant due to the clear definition of income ranges.", "Seema argued that Cohort 3 represents a specific segment within the premium segment, characterized by a focus on sustainability.", "Discussion of how the 3% of the population represented by Cohort 3 would be distributed among the premium segments.", "Agreement that Cohort 4 is also likely a subset of Cohort 1, representing a specific segment with different purchasing power.", "Seema suggested calculating Cohorts 3, 4, and 5 based on the combined size of Cohorts 1 and 2."], "end_time": "31:02", "start_time": "26:46", "time_range": "26:46 - 31:02", "participants": []}, {"topic": "Calculating Cohort Sizes & Proportions", "points": ["Discussion on how to calculate the sizes of Cohorts 3, 4, and 5 based on the combined size of Cohorts 1 and 2.", "Seema proposed calculating the total universe for Cohorts 3, 4, and 5 as the sum of the sizes of Cohorts 1 and 2.", "Agreement that the percentage representing each cohort would be calculated based on the total combined size.", "Discussion on how to distribute the 3% represented by Cohort 3 among the premium segments.", "Seema clarified that the goal is to refine the calculations to accurately represent the target audience and their purchasing power."], "end_time": "36:15", "start_time": "31:03", "time_range": "31:03 - 36:15", "participants": []}, {"topic": "Premium Investor Cohort Estimation & Refinement", "points": ["Seema is estimating percentages for aspirational premium investors and value maximization seekers.", "Difficulty in accurately estimating these segments within the premium market.", "Concern about lack of data for conscious lifestyle adopters (sustainability-driven).", "Discussion of potential missing filters in the initial guesstimation model.", "Identification of the 8% income segment (earning over 10 lakhs) as a key area for refinement.", "Rohan points out a potential error in the guesstimation logic related to the income segment.", "Rohit was involved in previous discussions about the guesstimation process.", "Seema is attempting to incorporate male representation into the cohort estimations.", "Seema emphasizes the importance of understanding the questions that will be asked to confidently answer them.", "Seema believes thorough investigation and multiple angles are crucial for accurate estimations.", "Seema is trying to validate the estimations and identify potential overestimations.", "Discussion of adding male segment data to improve accuracy."], "end_time": "39:50", "start_time": "36:17", "time_range": "36:17 - 39:50", "participants": ["Seema Samanta", "Rohan Kumar"]}, {"topic": "Brand Analysis - GTM Strategies & Marketplace Expansion", "points": ["Seema presented analysis of brands like Minimalist, Derma Co, Dr. Sheth's, Foxtail, Aqualogica, Deconstruct, Pilgrim, and Arthrism.", "These brands heavily utilized educational content and positioned themselves as scientifically/clinically approved.", "They prioritized building trust through lab tests and clinical approvals over aspirational marketing.", "Emphasis on transparency and clarity regarding ingredients.", "Brands initially focused on building an online presence (D2C).", "Once consumer networks were established, they expanded to offline channels and marketplaces.", "Discussion of whether brands توصیه marketplace expansion (Amazon, Myntra) or focused on D2C first.", "Seema is investigating why brands pivoted to marketplaces.", "Seema questions whether marketplace listings provide better ROI than Google/Meta advertising.", "Seema is looking for data to determine if D2C followed by marketplace expansion or immediate marketplace presence is more effective.", "Seema wants to understand if being available on popular shopping platforms is more beneficial.", "Seema is seeking information about which channels these brands prioritized for marketing and growth."], "end_time": "43:15", "start_time": "39:51", "time_range": "39:51 - 43:15", "participants": ["Seema Samanta", "Rohan Kumar"]}, {"topic": "Incubator & Grant Analysis - Next Steps", "points": ["Rohan wants to align with Maryam on the analysis of incubators.", "Rohan reference