nt the aspirational premium segment.\\n  • Reference to a survey indicating 80% of the population has a PCI (per capita income) around 1.2-1.3 lakhs.\\n  • Discussion of the validity of the 80% PCI figure, acknowledging the vast income disparity in India.\\n  • Emphasis on the importance of disposable income as a better indicator of purchasing power.\\n  • Debate on whether to adjust the percentages based on new data and ensure mutually exclusive cohorts.\\n  • Seema wants to define the percentage of people earning between 5-15 lakhs.\\n  • Reference to a survey showing 7-8% of the population has income above 10 lakhs and 70-72% below 5 lakhs.\\n  •  regering to the article stating that only the top 10% of the population falls in the income bracket wherein their net worth is more than 25 lakh rupees.\\n  • Discussion about the difference between net worth and disposable income.\\n  • Harsh offered to share census data on income.\\n  • Rohan emphasizes the importance of defining a smaller, well-defined cohort for targeted marketing.\\n  • Seema wants to determine the percentage of people with income above 15 lakhs.\\n  • Discussion about the importance of defining mutually exclusive cohorts for accurate targeting.\\n\\n[22:28 - 26:38] 📌 Initial Cohort Guesstimation & Income Disparity\\n  • Discussion began with questioning the accuracy of income percentages, specifically the claim that 80% of the population earns less than 8,000 rupees, deeming it potentially too high.\\n  • Seema presented initial data breakdown: 80-85% earning under 5 lakhs, 7-8% between 5-10 lakhs, and 8-10% earning over 10 lakhs.\\n  • Concern raised about the reliability of the data, with a need to verify the numbers against current statistics.\\n  • Discussion of adjusting income brackets, specifically changing the 15% earning over 10 lakhs to 20% and the 60% bracket to 25儚 to better represent female representation in the cohorts.\\n  • Reference to articles stating 80% of Indians earn less than 15,000 per month, emphasizing the need for source verification and understanding the calculation methods.\\n  • Acknowledgement that data is potentially 5 years old, necessitating updated information.\\n  • Harsh asked for clarification on the data's meaning and understanding of the income brackets.\\n  • Discussion of the median income in India being around 18,000.\\n  • Concerns about picking numbers arbitrarily and the need for supporting data.\\n  • Seema clarified that the goal is to refine the income distribution to better reflect the target audience.\\n\\n[26:46 - 31:02] 📌 Defining Cohort Characteristics & Overlap\\n  • Discussion of the characteristics defining each cohort, focusing on affluent, sustainability-driven, and impact-oriented consumers.\\n  • Seema clarified that the 'Conscious Lifestyle Adopter' (Cohort 3) is potentially a subset of the affluent (Cohort 1) and regular customers (Cohort 2).\\n  • Rohan suggested that Cohort 3 might be becoming redundant due to the clear definition of income ranges.\\n  • Seema argued that Cohort 3 represents a specific segment within the premium segment, characterized by a focus on sustainability.\\n  • Discussion of how the 3% of the population represented by Cohort 3 would be distributed among the premium segments.\\n  • Agreement that Cohort 4 is also likely a subset of Cohort 1, representing a specific segment with different purchasing power.\\n  • Seema suggested calculating Cohorts 3, 4, and 5 based on the combined size of Cohorts 1 and 2.\\n\\n[31:03 - 36:15] 📌 Calculating Cohort Sizes & Proportions\\n  • Discussion on how to calculate the sizes of Cohorts 3, 4, and 5 based on the combined size of Cohorts 1 and 2.\\n  • Seema proposed calculating the total universe for Cohorts 3, 4, and 5 as the sum of the sizes of Cohorts 1 and 2.\\n  • Agreement that the percentage representing each cohort would be calculated based on the total combined size.\\n  • Discussion on how to distribute the 3% represented by 