n leads to inaccurate speaker boundaries.\\n  • Whisper generates segments based on time, not speaker changes.\\n  • The problem is compounded by long periods of silence.\\n  • The current approach lacks accurate speaker boundary detection.\\n\\n[07:26 - 08:57] 📌 Speaker Diarization - Proposed Solution with PNA and Stack Data Structure\\n  👥 Key participants: Rohan Kumar, Ginish Garg, Chayan Roy\\n  • Ginish proposes using PNA (Pyannote Audio) for speaker diarization.\\n  • PNA can identify speaker changes with millisecond-level precision.\\n  • The idea is to chunk audio segments based on these speaker changes.\\n  • A stack data structure is proposed to manage speaker events.\\n  • When a speaker starts, an event is pushed onto the stack.\\n  • When a speaker ends, the event is popped from the stack.\\n  • This allows for accurate separation of audio segments based on speaker turns.\\n  • Discussion about handling overlapping speaker events.\\n  • The stack approach helps identify and separate overlapping speech.\\n  • Discussion about adding padding to the audio segments to mitigate errors.\\n  • The goal is to minimize inaccuracies in the transcript due to overlapping speech.\\n\\n[08:59 - 11:03] 📌 Technical Details of Speaker Diarization - Precision and Error Handling\\n  👥 Key participants: Rohan Kumar, Chayan Roy\\n  • Discussion about the precision of speaker change detection (millisecond vs. microsecond).\\n  • Chayan clarifies it's millisecond-level precision.\\n  • Rohan emphasizes the importance of handling overlapping speech.\\n  • Discussion about potential errors due to overlapping speech and how to minimize them.\\n  • Padding is proposed to mitigate errors and ensure accurate transcription.\\n  • The goal is to ensure that the LLM can understand the transcript despite minor errors.\\n\\n[21:33 - 23:33] 📌 PEC Collaboration & Resource Constraints\\n  👥 Key participants: Rohan Kumar, Ganesh\\n  • Rohan inquires about Ganesh's progress in finding collab