unspecified\\n\\n================================================================================\\n📝 MEETING SUMMARY (BY TOPIC)\\n================================================================================\\n\\n[00:03 - 02:35] 📌 Speaker Diarization and Transcript Alignment - Initial Explanation\\n  • Ginish presented documentation on implementing a structure for accurate speaker diarization and transcript alignment.\\n  • The current timeline involves speaker events (duration 10-25 seconds) and Whisper outputs (complete segment).\\n  • The issue is that Whisper outputs the entire segment, potentially misattributing speech to the wrong speaker.\\n  • Rohan explained the mismatch arises because Whisper doesn't separate overlapping speech.\\n  • Pioneer audio will be used to identify who spoke and when.\\n  • Whisper will provide *what* was said.\\n  • DOM events will map speaker names.\\n  • The goal is to reconstruct sentences based on speaker boundaries.\\n  • Ginish questioned the accuracy of Whisper's word-level timestamps.\\n  • Rohan described the process of normalizing segments and merging segments belonging to the same speaker.\\n  • The plan involves using word-level timestamps to create separate sentences for pianta audio.\\n  • The final step is mapping speaker names from DOM events.\\n  • Rohan asked Ginish to verify the accuracy of word-level timestamps.\\n\\n[02:38 - 05:10] 📌 Detailed Explanation of the Diarization Process\\n  • Rohan explained that Pioneer audio will generate speaker segments.\\n  • The system will attempt to separate overlapping speech.\\n  • Word-level timestamps from Whisper will be used to reconstruct sentences.\\n  • Sentences will be created based on speaker boundaries.\\n  • The system will assign names to speakers based on DOM events.\\n  • Rohan raised concerns about the accuracy of Whisper's timestamps, specifically the duration of words.\\n  • He questioned whether a word took 8.8 seconds to say.\\n  • Rohan suggested verifying the timestamps after transcript level.\\n  • The goal is to achieve accurate speaker separation, even with overlapping speech.\\n\\n[05:11 - 07:32] 📌 Concerns and Questions Regarding Merging and Accuracy\\n  • Rohan asked about the merging process, specifically how to handle the transition between speakers.\\n  • Ginish expressed concern about the merging section, particularly how to match data from different sources.\\n  • Ginish highlighted the challenge of matching finite details from the output with the high-level speaker names provided in the structure.\\n  • He questioned how to ensure everything aligns correctly, even if a fraction of a sentence is missed.\\n  • Ginish asked about a solid plan for handling this, specifically with Guinness.\\n  • Guinness will provide high-level speaker names and timestamps.\\n  • The output will contain more finite details about the speakers.\\n  • The challenge is merging these details accurately.\\n  • Chayan suggested manual checking after transcript level to verify speaker accuracy.\\n  • Ginish emphasized the need for a clear and solid approach.\\n\\n[07:33 - 11:31] 📌 Ginish's Availability and Next Steps\\n  • Discussion about Ginish's availability next week.\\n  • Ginish will be returning on Thursday.\\n  • He will continue working on Friday.\\n  • Rohan will check the Pianot TXT to verify the data.\\n  • Ginish asked Rohan to verify the larger time gaps between speakers to ensure they match the correct audio chunk.\\n\\n[11:32 - 13:17] 📌 Speaker Event/Transcript Timing Discrepancies\\n  👥 Key participants: Rohan Kumar, Ginish Garg\\n  • Rohan mentioned needing to match DOM event timings with speaker timings from Pianot audio to identify discrepancies.\\n  • Ginish suggested comparing the timings side-by-side to understand the differences.\\n  • The goal is to understand the magnitude of the timing differences and determine if they are significant enough to address.\\n  • The focus is on ensuring major speaker 