rrently include Meta ads (date, spend, impression, click).\\n  • Question about why Google Ads data isn't currently included.\\n  • Focus is solely on Instagram data for this initial dashboard.\\n  • The dashboard is being built in Superset.\\n  • Emphasis on justifying each visualization with both technical and business rationale.\\n  • Need to define formulas and explain the Y-axis for each chart.\\n  • Rohan wants Ayush and the team to understand how to interpret the data and identify actionable insights.\\n  • Discussion of dunno the 500 data points captured by Meta and how to prioritize which metrics matter.\\n  • Importance of choosing appropriate metrics (cumulative vs. average) for analysis.\\n  • The dashboard is a response to an immediate need and will be followed by a more detailed planning phase.\\n  • Documentation will be created for each dashboard to explain the rationale behind each visualization.\\n  • Focus on understanding the data, not just presenting it.\\n  • Discussion on how to approach the data and how to choose the right metrics for analysis.\\n\\n[16:41 - 19:25] 📌 Data Sharing and Format - JSON and Training Data\\n  • Esha Joshi will share data in JSON format.\\n  • The data will be used for training purposes.\\n  • Discussion about vectorizing the JSON data.\\n  • Esha Joshi needs to verify the data once it's shared.\\n  • The team is working on data compression and wants to share the results.\\n  • The data will be used for machine learning or training purposes.\\n  • Esha Joshi will verify the data once it's shared.\\n\\n[19:26 - 20:40] 📌 Image Compression - WebP and Tools\\n  • Chayan Roy has been exploring image compression tools.\\n  • Tools tested include Vips and PeeLo.\\n  • Vips is a Linux package managed tool with a Python component.\\n  • PeeLo is also a Python environment tool.\\n  • Discussion of using WebP as a compression format.\\n  • Dhruvik will evaluate the compression quality of different tools.\\n  • Chayan Roy will share the compressed files with Dhruvik for review.\\n  • Discussion about whether Shopify accepts WebP or converts JPEG to WebP.\\n  • Question about the possibility of converting images to SVG.\\n  • SVG is suitable for vector images, not captured images.\\n  • The goal is to achieve lossless compression while maintaining image quality.\\n  • Discussion about evaluating the compression ratio and file size reduction.\\n\\n[20:41 - 23:33] 📌 WebP vs. SVG - Image Types and Compression\\n  • Discussion on the differences between WebP and SVG.\\n  • WebP is suitable for raster images, while SVG is for vector images.\\n  • SVG is best for illustrations, not captured images.\\n  • The team is looking for lossless compression that maintains image quality.\\n  • They are evaluating different tools to achieve the desired compression ratio.\\n  • Emphasis on visual inspection and Dhruvik's expertise in evaluating compression quality.\\n  • The goal is to reduce file size without sacrificing image quality.\\n\\n[23:34 - 24:51] 📌 Image Compression & Format Discussion\\n  👥 Key participants: Rohan Kumar, Chayan Roy\\n  • Chayan Roy suggests a compression quality of 80-85 to preserve images.\\n  • Rohan Kumar asks Chayan to share a link to internal code group for settings.\\n  • Discussion about preferred image format: WebP vs. JPEG.\\n  • Rohan Kumar wants to know if Chayan needs to ask about WebP or JPEG preference.\\n  • Chayan Roy confirms code is ready to run for renaming ingredients.\\n  • Discussion about compressing all images and folders.\\n  • Rohan Kumar specifies pre-pick images are already large (4-5MB).\\n  • Target file size is ideally under 2MB, aiming for 1MB.\\n  • Rohan Kumar emphasizes maintaining image quality during compression.\\n  • Target compression quality is 85-90, avoiding significant quality loss.\\n  • Discussion about maintaining resolution (around 1200-2k).\\n  • Chayan Roy suggests reducing color profile to reduce file size.\\n  • Rohan Kumar ques