n of accessing per-user audio streams in Google Meet.\\n  • Google Meet doesn't officially allow access to these streams.\\n  • Exploring the possibility of injecting a JavaScript script to tap into streams via WebRTC neuralgia.\\n  • Concerns about the scalability of this approach if it were to be implemented.\\n  • The potential for UI changes in Google Meet to break any implemented solution.\\n  • Ginish suggests focusing on vector embeddings as the primary approach due to scalability concerns.\\n  • Chayan acknowledges the difficulty of scaling the direct audio stream access method.\\n  • Exploring the idea of a backup plan using the JavaScript injection method for testing purposes.\\n  • Discussion of whether accessing individual audio streams is worth the effort given the potential complexities.\\n  • Ginish emphasizes that if UI changes occur, maintaining the solution would be difficult.\\n  • Acknowledging that a JavaScript injection approach might be a viable short-term solution for testing.\\n  • Chayan suggests that if the vector embedding approach works, the direct audio stream access can be revisited later.\\n  • Discussion of the potential for network glitches to affect the reliability of audio stream access.\\n  • Ginish and Chayan agree that focusing on the vector embedding approach is the most practical path forward.\\n\\n[02:12 - 06:10] 📌 Vector Embeddings for User Identification\\n  • Discussion of using vector embeddings to identify users based on their audio characteristics.\\n  • Chayan outlines a process for generating per-user embeddings after each meeting.\\n  • The plan involves creating a registry of user embeddings and using DOM events to match new users.\\n  • The goal is to automate the mapping process and reduce manual dependencies.\\n  • Chayan suggests using DOM events to identify the most likely user associated with a given embedding.\\n  • Ginish emphasizes the importance of relying solely on DOM events for initial mapping.\\n  • Discussion of how to handle outliers in the embedding space.\\n  • Ginish believes that the embeddings generated by PyAnode are likely to form clusters, minimizing the outlier problem.\\n  • Chayan will conduct a vector search to determine the appropriate cutoff for identifying matching users.\\n  • Chayan will prepare a detailed plan for implementing the vector embedding process by tomorrow.\\n  • The plan involves using a vector database (pgVector) to store the embeddings and DOM events for matching.\\n  • Discussion of the need for a database to store the audio embeddings and DOM event data.\\n  • Chayan will explore the use of pgVector for storing the vector embeddings.\\n\\n[06:12 - 08:59] 📌 Resume Review & Potential Candidate\\n  • Ginish mentions receiving a resume from a college friend.\\n  • The friend expressed interest in a project.\\n  • Discussion of how the candidate was identified and whether they proactively applied or were referred.\\n  • Ginish explains that he mentioned the project to his friend, who then shared the resume.\\n  • The conversation briefly touches on the candidate's qualifications and potential fit for the team.\\n  • The topic is quickly transitioned, suggesting it's a minor point in the overall discussion.\\n  • The conversation suggests a casual approach to recruitment, relying on personal networks.\\n\\n[09:01 - 11:36] 📌 Task Reminder Tab & UI Updates\\n  • Shubhang mentions adding a button for search tasks and a calendar option.\\n  • The new button allows users to search tasks based on a grid.\\n  • Clicking the reminder section auto-fills fields.\\n  • Currently, there's no specific department assigned to Rohan.\\n  • The UI allows users to write messages and send them directly to individuals.\\n  • The system displays the department if a specific department is selected.\\n  • Shubhang is addressing an issue where the UI resets after sending a message.\\n  • Discussion of whether the task reminder tab is still necessary given 