  • Yogita requests a draft version (V1) of the reel by Wednesday morning for review and potential changes.\\n  • The goal is to have the reel finalized by Friday morning.\\n  • Discussion about the overall timeline and ensuring enough time for revisions.\\n\\n[15:51 - 17:09] 📌 Travel & Catch-Up on Discussions\\n  • Mention of Aakash and Akshara being away on travel.\\n  • Plan to discuss topics with them when they return.\\n  • Indication that the current discussion is focused on those present on the call.\\n\\n[17:11 - 18:45] 📌 Onboarding & Access to Tools\\n  • Rohan Kumar confirms access to common email IDs (design@christineforest, designteam.pf@gmail.com).\\n  • Confirmation of access to FreePicker.\\n  • Instruction to reach out in the Town Square group for any issues with office infrastructure or tools.\\n  • General onboarding information for new team members.\\n\\n[18:45 - 19:32] 📌 Introduction of Sanjeev & UX Discussion\\n  • Introduction of Sanjeev.\\n  • Rohan Kumar expresses a need to understand Sanjeev's UX background and past work.\\n  • Indication that this is a new area for Rohan and a desire to learn from Sanjeev's expertise.\\n\\n[19:33 - 22:09] 📌 Project Introduction & Background (Food Companion App)\\n  👥 Key participants: Rohan Kumar, Sanjhi Jain\\n  • Rohan introduces the food companion app designed for Gen Z and millennials to solve the daily 'what to eat' decision problem.\\n  • The app uses conversational AI and research to understand user problems.\\n  • Focus is on solving the problem of daily meal decisions for young adults and those entering the workforce.\\n  • The app aims to address issues like boredom with repetitive meals, lack of time, and the desire for healthy eating.\\n  • Rohan describes the initial design process starting with user research.\\n  • The app aims to solve the daily meal decision problem for young adults and those entering the workforce.\\n  • Rohan mentions a focus on the psychology of food choices and decision-making.\\n  • The app aims to solve the daily meal decision problem for young adults and those entering the workforce.\\n  • Rohan mentions a focus on the psychology of food choices and decision-making.\\n\\n[22:09 - 24:34] 📌 User Research Methodology (Quantitative & Qualitative)\\n  👥 Key participants: Rohan Kumar, Sanjhi Jain\\n  • Rohan details the use of both quantitative (Google Forms surveys with 50-70 respondents, 10 questions) and qualitative (10-15 in-person interviews) research methods.\\n  • Qualitative research focused on understanding the underlying reasons for user difficulties with meal planning.\\n  • The research explored aspects like ingredient availability, psychological factors, and decision-跏making processes.\\n  • Rohan used the UXR Field Guide to formulate research questions.\\n  • Rohan used a Miro board and affinity mapping to analyze the research data and identify key themes.\\n  • Key themes identified included ingredient availability, difficulty following recipes while cooking, budget constraints, and a desire for variety.\\n  • Rohan explains the process of gathering and analyzing research data.\\n  • Emphasis on understanding the psychological aspects of meal planning.\\n  • Discussion of the challenges of balancing healthy eating with time and budget constraints.\\n\\n[24:34 - 25:37] 📌 App Features & Functionality (Scan My Fridge, Personalized Recipes)\\n  👥 Key participants: Rohan Kumar, Sanjhi Jain\\n  • Rohan describes a 'Scan My Fridge' feature that allows users to input their existing ingredients.\\n  • The app will then curate recipes based on the user's available ingredients, reducing waste and cost.\\n  • The app aims to provide personalized recipe recommendations based on user preferences, dietary restrictions, and available ingredients.\\n  • The app aims to address the issue of food waste by suggesting recipes that utilize existing ingredients.\\n  • Discussion of the importance of tailoring recipe recommendations to individual user needs and preferences.\\n  • The app will curate recipes based on the user's available ingredients, reducing waste and cost.\\n  • Discussion of the importance of tailoring recipe recommendations to individual user needs and preferences.\\n  • The app aims to provide personalized recipe recommendations based on user preferences, dietary restrictions, and available ingredients.\\n\\n[25:37 - 27:44] 📌 Competitive Analysis & Differentiation (Samsung Food)\\n  👥 Key participants: Rohan Kumar, Sanjhi Jain\\n  • Rohan mentions Samsung Food as a competitor.\\n  • The key differentiator is personalization and customization of recipe recommendations.\\n  • Samsung Food lacks the ability to tailor recipes to individual user preferences and available ingredients.\\n  • The app aims to provide a more personalized and customized experience compared to existing solutions.\\n  • Rohan discusses the importance of understanding the competitive landscape.\\n  • The app aims to provide a more personalized and customized experience compared to existing solutions.\\n  • Rohan discusses the importance of understanding the competitive landscape.\\n\\n[27:45 - 29:36] 📌 User Interview & Questioning Techniques\\n  👥 Key participants: Rohan Kumar, Sanjhi Jain\\n  • Sanjhi asks Rohan about the specific questions used in the user interviews.\\n  • Discussion of the importance of asking the right questions to gather meaningful insights.\\n  • Sanjhi inquires about the number of participants in the interviews and the overall research process.\\n  • Sanjhi probes Rohan about the information he already had before starting the research.\\n  • Discussion of the importance of understanding user needs and preferences.\\n  • Sanjhi asks about the research process and the specific questions asked to users.\\n  • Discussion of the importance of understanding the competitive landscape.\\n\\n[29:37 - 30:57] 📌 Introduction & Background - Sanjhi Jain's Project\\n  👥 Key participants: Sanjhi Jain, Roh